Message catalog maintenance
===========================

Each program's nls.json contains its catalog name, extraction inputs,
keywords, and format flags.  Edit these files directly; there are no
generated adapters or metadata regeneration steps.

File names are relative to the catalog directory.  @source@ denotes the
source root; @name@ includes a list from src/nls-common.json, which holds
shared frontend and backend settings.  Keep file order stable: it affects
catalog order.  The backend uses a scan list of directories searched
recursively for C sources and proctypelist.h instead of individual files.

Make reads the JSON through read-nls.pl using Perl's core JSON::PP module.
The reader returns Make assignments in memory, and errors stop Make.
Catalog templates depend on both the local and common JSON files.  PGXS
installs the reader and common metadata and accepts either nls.json or
legacy nls.mk files in external extensions.  If both exist, nls.json wins.

Each catalog directory registers itself in po_catalogs in its meson.build,
with the generated inputs needed for extraction.  Optional languages
register only when enabled.  Meson tracks the local JSON files so changing
a catalog name triggers reconfiguration.  Other metadata is read on every
maintenance invocation.  Build dependencies belong in the respective
Makefiles and meson.build files, not in the JSON metadata.

maintain_po.py implements the Meson init-po and update-po targets without
Make or Autoconf.  It writes templates and .po.new files into the build
tree and leaves source translations untouched.  WANTED_LANGUAGES limits
the update operation to languages in its space-separated value.  An unset
or empty value processes all languages found in source catalogs; codes
with no source catalog are ignored.

Each output file is replaced only after its gettext command succeeds.  If
an operation fails, earlier successful outputs are retained; the operation
as a whole is not atomic.

The backend file search deliberately matches Make's source-tree search,
including the spelling and ordering of paths.  In an out-of-tree build
that search does not include generated build-tree sources.  Changing that
coverage should be done separately for both build systems.
